      },
      {
        "elements": [
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 4.415011037527593,
            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Patron of the Arts</strong><br>\nPericles also contributed to the cultural bloom in Athens. He was a patron of the arts and literature, believing that cultural excellence was a sign of a city's greatness. Under his leadership, Athens produced some of the most notable art and architecture in Greek history, including the Parthenon and works by the playwrights Sophocles and Euripides.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Plague of Athens</strong><br>\nDuring the Peloponnesian War, a great plague struck Athens, killing a significant portion of the population. This plague was a turning point in Pericles's political life as well as in Athenian history. Despite his personal grief—Pericles lost both his sisters and his sons to the plague—he continued to lead his city with conviction.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Pericles' Leadership Style</strong><br>\nPericles was known for his persuasive oratory and his clear vision for Athens. He was not a tyrant; instead, he believed in the will of the majority and led through powerful speeches that encouraged Athenian citizens to follow his policies. He always pushed for building a society that was fair and beneficial for all Athenians.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
