          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>The Peloponnesian War</strong><br>\nUnder Pericles's leadership, Athens entered the Peloponnesian War against Sparta and its allies. The war was long and devastating, with many shifts in power. Pericles's death in 429 BC came before the end of the war, which eventually concluded with an Athenian defeat and a significant reshaping of the Greek world.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Periclean Building Program</strong><br>\nPericles initiated a massive building program in Athens, which was financed by the Delian League's treasury. This program not only beautified the city but also provided employment to thousands of Athenians. The Acropolis, with its architectural masterpieces, stands as a lasting testament to his vision and the skills of Athenian workers and artists.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Pericles' Legacy</strong><br>\nThe era under Pericles's rule is often referred to as the \"Golden Age of Athens\" because of the unprecedented progress in the arts, philosophy, and architecture. His impact on Athens's political and cultural life continued to be felt long after his death, shaping the Western political and cultural landscape for centuries to come.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3d655444-fd55-42f7-aba2-16a16db7dc58"
